# The central architectural rule

This is the thing I'd put in the constitution:

> **Generative models may propose visual reality. Only the canon system may establish it.**

And:

> **Once visual reality is established, downstream generators reference it rather than reinventing it.**

That gives RoboBladez an accumulating world instead of an endless stream of disconnected AI generations.

Over time you end up with:

```text
100 Agents
+
300 body versions
+
80 Daimons
+
40 arenas/worlds
+
200 effect grammars
+
thousands of state assets
```

all connected to actual competition history.

At that point the visual library itself becomes a huge persistent IP asset — and the autonomous production system can create new episodes largely by resolving and recombining canonical objects rather than designing the world from scratch every time.
